<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Johann I Joseph, Prince of Liechtenstein</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1760">1760</a>–<a href="/years/1836">1836</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-liechtenstein">House of Liechtenstein</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Johann I Joseph was prince of Liechtenstein from 1805 to 1836 and an Austrian field marshal who fought in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleonic-wars">Napoleonic Wars</a> and negotiated the Peace of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bratislava">Pressburg</a> in 1805. Under him Liechtenstein became sovereign in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/confederation-of-the-rhine">Confederation of the Rhine</a> and then joined the German Confederation; he issued its first constitution in 1818.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/johann-i-joseph-prince-of-liechtenstein.jpg" alt="Johann I Joseph, Prince of Liechtenstein" width="320" height="403" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Johann Baptist von Lampi the Elder · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Johann_Josef_I_von_Liechtenstein.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Johann Baptist Josef Adam Johann Nepomuk Aloys Franz de Paula was born on 26 June 1760, the fourth son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/franz-joseph-i">Franz Joseph I</a>, Prince of Liechtenstein, and Countess Leopoldine von Sternberg. His two eldest brothers died young. The next eldest, Aloys I, Prince of Liechtenstein, died childless in 1805, which brought Johann to the throne. At the age of 21 he entered the army as a lieutenant in a cuirassier regiment, and in 1785 he joined the Freemason lodge in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>During the Austro-Turkish War of 1788-1791 Johann was promoted rapidly to colonel and gained a name as a cavalry officer. He received the Knight's Cross of the Military Order of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maria-theresa">Maria Theresa</a> in 1790. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-revolutionary-wars">French Revolutionary Wars</a> he took part in a cavalry action at Avesnes-le-Sec on 12 September 1793, in which Republican troops lost 2,000 killed and wounded and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/allies">Allies</a> only 69 men.</p><p>He fought at the Battle of Fleurus after promotion to General-Major in June 1794, and in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-of-the-second-coalition">War of the Second Coalition</a> he commanded the Austrian Reserve at the Battle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trebbia">Trebbia</a> and a cavalry division at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-hohenlinden">Battle of Hohenlinden</a> on 3 December 1800.</p><p>At the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-austerlitz">Battle of Austerlitz</a> he led the 4,600 cavalry of the 5th Column but could not save the Austrian-Russian army from defeat. He then negotiated the Peace of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bratislava">Pressburg</a> with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a>. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-of-the-fifth-coalition">War of the Fifth Coalition</a> he commanded the I Reserve Korps under Archduke Charles at Eckmühl, Aspern-Essling and Wagram. When the archduke resigned he took command of the main army until the end of 1809, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francis-ii">Emperor Francis II</a> promoted him Feldmarschall in September. He negotiated and signed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-schonbrunn">Peace of Schönbrunn</a>, which, like Pressburg, was hard on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a>. He was afterwards accused of little diplomatic skill and resigned from the army in 1810.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Johann ruled as Prince of Liechtenstein from 1805 to 1806 and again from 1814 until 1836, and was regent from 1806 until 1814. In 1806 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a> incorporated Liechtenstein in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/confederation-of-the-rhine">Confederation of the Rhine</a> and made it a sovereign state. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/congress-of-vienna">Congress of Vienna</a> approved its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sovereignty">sovereignty</a>, and in 1815 it became a member of the German Confederation.</p><p>Johann made forward-looking reforms but governed in an absolutist manner. In 1818 he granted a limited constitution. He expanded agriculture and forestry, reorganised his administration, and laid out <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/biedermeier">Biedermeier</a> gardens and parks on the English model. He married Landgravine Josefa of Fürstenberg-Weitra in 1792, and they had fourteen children. He died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a> on 20 April 1836 and was succeeded by his eldest son, Aloys II.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Johann I Joseph of Liechtenstein” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Prince <b>Johann I</b> issued a constitution based on the estates in 1818, and Johann II granted a constitution in 1862 that created an elected Landtag.</blockquote><a href="/empire/principality-of-liechtenstein">Principality of Liechtenstein · Governance &amp; Law</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/austria">Austria</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/austria">Austria</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=48.21,16.37&amp;year=1809" aria-label="Vienna on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4931 477 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Johann I Joseph, Prince of Liechtenstein"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5381 747h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5381 747h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=48.21,16.37&amp;year=1809">What was here in 1809? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
